    Ok(self.peeked.as_ref().map(|t| match t {
        Tok::Num(v, p) => Tok::Num(*v, *p),
        t => t.clone(),
    }))
}
```

This is garbage — leftover debris from a draft. `self.peeked` is `Option<Tok>`, not `Option<Result<...>>`. Let me rewrite peek cleanly:

```rust
fn peek(&mut self) -> Result<Option<Tok>, Error> {
    if self.peeked.is_none() {
        self.peeked = self.lexer.next_token()?;
    }
    Ok(self.peeked.clone())
}

fn advance(&mut self) -> Result<Option<Tok>, Error> {
    let t = self.peek()?;
    self.peeked = None;
    Ok(t)
}
```

This is much simpler — peek returns an owned Tok (cloned), and advance consumes it. Let me fix this.

