There are two problems with the test file:
1. The `UnwrapErr` trait has an associated type named `Ok`, which conflicts with the `Ok` variant of the enum... actually, E0437 says that the type `Ok` is not a member of the trait — this is because of the `Self::Ok` syntax: an associated type named `Ok`... the real problem is `type Ok = Error;` — hmm, E0437 "type Ok is not a member of trait UnwrapErr". This is strange. Ah, I get it — it's because within the trait `UnwrapErr` I declared `fn unwrap_err_or_panic(self, src: &str) -> Self::Ok;`, but I didn't declare `type Ok;` inside the trait! I wrote `type Ok = Error;` in the impl, but the trait side only has `fn unwrap_err_or_panic(self, src: &str) -> Self::Ok;` without `type Ok;`. Let me check what I actually wrote:

```rust
trait UnwrapErr {
    fn unwrap_err_or_panic(self, src: &str) -> Self::Ok;
}
```

Yeah — I forgot `type Ok;` in the trait definition. Simpler: just remove the trait entirely and make `err` a plain function that returns an Error.

2. `with_vars`: `pairs.iter().copied()` — pairs is `&[(&str, f64)]`, so `.iter().copied()` returns `(&str, f64)`. Collecting into a HashMap requires `(String, f64)`. Fix: `.iter().map(|(k, v)| (k.to_string(), *v)).collect()`.

Let me rewrite the helpers cleanly.

